# Store and check out every text file with LF, on every platform.
#
# This must be the FIRST rule: Git applies the LAST matching pattern, so the
# binary and per-type exceptions below deliberately follow it.
#
# `*.sh text eol=lf` was added for bash, but the problem is not specific to
# shell. Git for Windows ships `core.autocrlf=true` system-wide, so every other
# tracked text file still smudges to CRLF on checkout while the Git object
# stays LF. Measured on a Windows checkout: `ui/src/app.css` carried 2336 CR
# bytes in the working tree and 0 in the object, and
# `ui/src/flagPolyfill.test.ts` — which asserts a `\n` literal against the file
# it reads at runtime — failed there and only there. Python, TypeScript and CSS
# were all unprotected: 186, 61 and 5 tracked files respectively.
#
# `text=auto` leaves content Git detects as binary alone, and the explicit
# exceptions below state that intent rather than relying on the detection.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Bytes, not text. Several are hash-pinned and any conversion breaks the hash.
*.png binary
*.gif binary
*.mp4 binary
*.webp binary
*.woff2 binary
*.wasm binary

# Use bd merge for beads JSONL files
.beads/issues.jsonl merge=beads

# Vendored viewer assets are verified by sha256 at export time (share.py).
# Without -text, git's autocrlf rewrites LF to CRLF on checkout under Windows,
# which changes the bytes and makes the checksum fail — measured: 5 of 6 assets
# differ from their index hash, and share_export raises ShareExportError before
# producing anything. Treat them as opaque bytes, because that is what they are.
src/mcp_agent_mail/viewer_assets/vendor/** -text
src/mcp_agent_mail/viewer_assets/coi-serviceworker.js -text

# The Windows flag fallback is hash-pinned by the UI build hook.  Keep Git from
# applying any text conversion before that byte-for-byte validation runs.
ui/public/assets/TwemojiCountryFlags.woff2 -text

# Every tracked .sh is executed by bash (hooks, integrators, deploy). Git for
# Windows ships core.autocrlf=true system-wide (GHA runners included), which
# smudges these to CRLF on checkout; bash then misparses them and tests that
# execute or copy them from the working tree fail on every Windows checkout.
*.sh text eol=lf
